<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>CastIQ RevOps Dashboard</title>
<meta name="description" content="RevOps analytics for an industrial die-cast supplier: market sizing, ICP scoring, territory design, RFQ win/loss, lead scoring, pipeline health, forecasting and data quality - built on public Hugging Face datasets.">
<style>
:root {
  color-scheme: light;
  --page: #f9f9f7; --surface: #fcfcfb; --ink: #0b0b0b; --ink2: #52514e; --muted: #898781;
  --grid: #e1e0d9; --axis: #c3c2b7; --ring: rgba(11,11,11,0.10);
  --series1: #2a78d6; --neutral: #b4b2a9; --pos: #2a78d6; --neg: #e34948;
  --ord1: #86b6ef; --ord2: #3987e5; --ord3: #256abf; --ord4: #104281;
  --seq0: #e6f0fc; --seq1: #b7d3f6; --seq2: #6da7ec; --seq3: #2a78d6; --seq4: #184f95;
  --tileink0: #0b0b0b; --tileink1: #0b0b0b; --tileink2: #0b0b0b; --tileink3: #ffffff; --tileink4: #ffffff;
  --good: #006300; --critical: #d03b3b; --accent-bg: #eef4fc; --real: #006300; --synth: #8a5a00;
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
    color-scheme: dark;
    --page: #0d0d0d; --surface: #1a1a19; --ink: #f0efec; --ink2: #c3c2b7; --muted: #898781;
    --grid: #2c2c2a; --axis: #383835; --ring: rgba(255,255,255,0.10);
    --series1: #3987e5; --neutral: #5b5a55; --pos: #3987e5; --neg: #e66767;
    --ord1: #184f95; --ord2: #256abf; --ord3: #3987e5; --ord4: #86b6ef;
    --seq0: #142235; --seq1: #184f95; --seq2: #256abf; --seq3: #3987e5; --seq4: #86b6ef;
    --tileink0: #f0efec; --tileink1: #f0efec; --tileink2: #ffffff; --tileink3: #0b0b0b; --tileink4: #0b0b0b;
    --good: #0ca30c; --critical: #e66767; --accent-bg: #15212f; --real: #0ca30c; --synth: #eda100;
  }
}
:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  color-scheme: dark;
  --page: #0d0d0d; --surface: #1a1a19; --ink: #f0efec; --ink2: #c3c2b7; --muted: #898781;
  --grid: #2c2c2a; --axis: #383835; --ring: rgba(255,255,255,0.10);
  --series1: #3987e5; --neutral: #5b5a55; --pos: #3987e5; --neg: #e66767;
  --ord1: #184f95; --ord2: #256abf; --ord3: #3987e5; --ord4: #86b6ef;
  --seq0: #142235; --seq1: #184f95; --seq2: #256abf; --seq3: #3987e5; --seq4: #86b6ef;
  --tileink0: #f0efec; --tileink1: #f0efec; --tileink2: #ffffff; --tileink3: #0b0b0b; --tileink4: #0b0b0b;
  --good: #0ca30c; --critical: #e66767; --accent-bg: #15212f; --real: #0ca30c; --synth: #eda100;
}
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
html, body { margin: 0; background: var(--page); color: var(--ink); }
body { font: 15px/1.55 system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif; }
.wrap { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 16px 64px; }
header.top { padding: 40px 0 8px; display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: flex-start; justify-content: space-between; }
h1 { font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 38px); line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 8px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
h2 { font-size: 22px; margin: 0 0 4px; }
h3 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; color: var(--ink2); font-weight: 600; }
p { margin: 0 0 10px; }
.lede { color: var(--ink2); max-width: 820px; font-size: 16px; }
.muted { color: var(--muted); }
button.theme { background: var(--surface); color: var(--ink); border: 1px solid var(--ring); border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 8px 12px; font: inherit; cursor: pointer; white-space: nowrap; }
nav.toc { position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 5; background: var(--page); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grid);
  display: flex; gap: 4px; overflow-x: auto; padding: 8px 0; margin-bottom: 8px; }
nav.toc a { color: var(--ink2); text-decoration: none; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px; white-space: nowrap; font-size: 14px; }
nav.toc a:hover { background: var(--accent-bg); color: var(--ink); }
.kpis { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(170px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin: 16px 0 8px; }
.kpi { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--ring); border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 16px; }
.kpi .v { font-size: 26px; font-weight: 650; line-height: 1.2; }
.kpi .l { color: var(--ink2); font-size: 13px; }
.kpi .s { color: var(--muted); font-size: 12px; margin-top: 4px; }
section { margin-top: 36px; }
.sec-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px 12px; align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: 12px; }
.badge { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; border-radius: 999px; padding: 2px 10px; border: 1px solid currentColor; }
.badge.real { color: var(--real); } .badge.synth { color: var(--synth); }
.grid2 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 16px; }
.grid3 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr; gap: 16px; }
@media (max-width: 860px) { .grid2, .grid3 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } header.top { flex-direction: column; } }
.card { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--ring); border-radius: 12px; padding: 16px; min-width: 0; }
.chart { width: 100%; min-height: 240px; }
.sowhat { background: var(--accent-bg); border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-top: 16px; }
.sowhat b { color: var(--ink); }
.sowhat ul { margin: 6px 0 0; padding-left: 20px; }
.tbl { overflow-x: auto; }
.tbl table { min-width: 560px; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 13px; }
th, td { text-align: left; padding: 7px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grid); vertical-align: top; }
th { color: var(--ink2); font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
td.num { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }
.ok { color: var(--good); font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; } .bad { color: var(--critical); font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
.legend { display: flex; gap: 14px; flex-wrap: wrap; font-size: 12px; color: var(--ink2); margin-top: 6px; }
.sw { display: inline-block; width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 3px; vertical-align: -2px; margin-right: 4px; }
code { font: 13px ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; background: var(--accent-bg); padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: 4px; }
footer { margin-top: 48px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 13px; border-top: 1px solid var(--grid); padding-top: 16px; }
a { color: var(--series1); }
</style>
<script>
try { const t = localStorage.getItem("castiq-theme"); if (t) document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t; } catch (e) {}
</script>
<script>{{ plotlyjs }}</script>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">
<header class="top">
  <div>
    <h1>CastIQ: the RevOps engine I would build for an industrial die-cast supplier</h1>
    <p class="lede">I sold industrial die-cast parts to manufacturers. This project turns that field knowledge into
    the systems a revenue team runs on: who to sell to, who owns which account, which RFQs to chase, which leads to call first,
    what the forecast really is, and which CRM data is broken. Everything is reproducible from pinned public Hugging Face datasets.</p>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px">Data: <span class="badge real">REAL</span> = real-world records ·
    <span class="badge synth">SYNTHETIC</span> = published simulated CRM (no real CRM is public). Every rate shows its sample size and 95% interval.</p>
  </div>
  <button class="theme" id="themeBtn" type="button" aria-label="Switch light or dark theme">◐ Theme</button>
</header>

<nav class="toc" aria-label="Sections">
  <a href="#market">1 · Market &amp; ICP</a><a href="#territories">2 · Territories</a><a href="#winloss">3 · RFQ win/loss</a>
  <a href="#leads">4 · Lead scoring</a><a href="#pipeline">5 · Pipeline</a><a href="#forecast">6 · Forecast</a>
  <a href="#quality">7 · Data quality</a><a href="#method">8 · Method</a>
</nav>

<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ m.market.in_market_accounts|num }}</div><div class="l">in-market US manufacturers scored</div><div class="s">from {{ m.market.country.country_companies|num }} US companies</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ m.market.tier_a_accounts|num }}</div><div class="l">Tier-A ICP accounts</div><div class="s">SAM {{ m.market.sam_usd|money }} / yr (illustrative)</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ "%.1f"|format(m.territories.max_min_before) }}× → {{ "%.2f"|format(m.territories.max_min_after) }}×</div><div class="l">territory imbalance (largest ÷ smallest)</div><div class="s">all territories contiguous</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ "%.2f"|format(m.win_loss.cv_auc_mean) }}</div><div class="l">RFQ win/loss model AUC</div><div class="s">top-quartile deals win {{ m.win_loss.q4_win_rate|pct }} vs {{ m.win_loss.q1_win_rate|pct }}</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">+{{ m.forecast.stage_default_overstatement|pct }}</div><div class="l">forecast overstated by CRM stage defaults</div><div class="s">{{ m.forecast.forecast_stage_default|money }} vs {{ m.forecast.forecast_model_expected|money }}</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{{ m.quality.failed_checks }} / {{ m.quality.checks }}</div><div class="l">data-quality checks failing</div><div class="s">with owner + fix for each</div></div>
</div>

<!-- 1 MARKET -->
<section id="market">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>1 · Market map &amp; ICP fit</h2><span class="badge real">REAL · 17M-company firmographics</span></div>
  <p class="lede">I filtered {{ m.market.country.country_companies|num }} US companies down to {{ m.market.in_market_accounts|num }} in industries that buy
  die-cast parts, then scored each one 0–100 on industry fit, size (tooling only pays back at volume) and freight distance from the
  Midwest/Southeast auto belt. The rules live in <code>config/icp.yaml</code>, so a sales leader can change them without code.</p>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card"><h3>Where the spend is: top industries by estimated die-cast spend</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-market_industries"></div></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Companies per ICP tier</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-market_tiers"></div>
      <table><tbody>
        <tr><td>TAM (all in-market)</td><td class="num">{{ m.market.tam_usd|money }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SAM (A–C tiers × serviceable share)</td><td class="num">{{ m.market.sam_usd|money }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SOM (3-yr obtainable)</td><td class="num">{{ m.market.som_usd|money }}</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:12px;margin-top:6px">$ values use editable spend-per-company assumptions; account counts are real.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>Top non-strategic target accounts (full list: <code>docs/data/top_accounts.csv</code>)</h3>{{ tables.top_accounts }}</div>
  <div class="sowhat"><b>So what:</b> {{ m.market.tier_ab_accounts|num }} A/B accounts is more than any field team can cover.
  The next two sections decide <i>who</i> works them and <i>which</i> opportunities deserve a quote.</div>
</section>

<!-- 2 TERRITORIES -->
<section id="territories">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>2 · Territory design &amp; routing</h2><span class="badge real">REAL · same firmographics</span></div>
  <p class="lede">Geography-only territories (k-means on state centres) were {{ "%.1f"|format(m.territories.max_min_before) }}× apart in potential —
  one rep would get a fortune, another a desert. I re-balanced with a local search that moves one border state at a time and never breaks
  a territory into pieces. Result: {{ "%.2f"|format(m.territories.max_min_after) }}× spread (coefficient of variation {{ "%.2f"|format(m.territories.cv_before) }} → {{ "%.2f"|format(m.territories.cv_after) }}).
  The {{ m.territories.strategic_accounts|num }} largest Tier-A OEMs ({{ m.territories.strategic_potential_usd|money }} potential) are carved out to a strategic team first.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h3>Territory map: tile colour = A/B potential, bold lines = territory borders</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-tile_map"></div>
      <div class="legend"><span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--seq0)"></span>low potential</span><span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--seq4)"></span>high potential</span><span>T1…T6 = territory</span></div></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Potential per territory: before (geography only) vs after (balanced, contiguous)</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-territory_balance"></div>{{ tables.territories }}</div>
  </div>
  <div class="sowhat"><b>Routing rules</b> (first match wins, in <code>routing.py</code>): ① Tier-A with 5K+ employees → Strategic team ·
  ② no usable state → RevOps data review ({{ m.territories.data_review_accounts|num }} accounts) · ③ state → territory → round-robin
  by fit score across {{ m.territories.reps }} reps, capped at {{ m.territories.named_accounts_per_rep }} named accounts each; the long tail goes to inside sales.
  Every account gets an owner <i>and</i> the rule that put it there — so a rep can never say "nobody told me".</div>
</section>

<!-- 3 WIN LOSS -->
<section id="winloss">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>3 · Which RFQs to chase: win/loss drivers</h2><span class="badge real">REAL · {{ m.win_loss.deals }} B2B deals</span></div>
  <p class="lede">The deals are from a B2B solutions seller, but the buying motion is the one I know from die casting: RFI → RFQ package →
  sometimes a formal tender, a purchasing department, competing quotes, a funded budget. A plain logistic regression on the qualification fields
  separates winners from losers with AUC {{ "%.2f"|format(m.win_loss.cv_auc_mean) }} ± {{ "%.2f"|format(m.win_loss.cv_auc_std) }} (5-fold CV, 20 repeats).</p>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card"><h3>Signals that move win rate (only statistically significant rows)</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-drivers"></div>
      <div class="legend"><span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--pos)"></span>raises win rate</span><span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--neg)"></span>lowers win rate</span></div></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Win rate by deal-score quartile</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-quartiles"></div>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Scores are out-of-fold: each deal was scored by a model that never saw it.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>The RFQ qualification checklist this produces</h3>{{ tables.checklist }}</div>
  <div class="sowhat"><b>So what:</b> the losers are not "bad luck". "Unknown" budget and "unknown" purchasing involvement lose far more often
  than either answer — <b>if the rep doesn't know, the deal is already in trouble.</b> New logos and competitive quotes win far less. In a die-cast shop,
  every RFQ costs engineering hours (DFM review, tooling quote, simulation). Quote the top quartile hard; ask the checklist questions before
  quoting the bottom quartile.</div>
</section>

<!-- 4 LEADS -->
<section id="leads">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>4 · Lead scoring with a leakage audit</h2><span class="badge synth">SYNTHETIC CRM · {{ m.lead_scoring.tier_used }} tier</span></div>
  <p class="lede">Model chosen on the validation split, then scored once on {{ m.lead_scoring.test_rows }} untouched test leads: AUC {{ "%.3f"|format(m.lead_scoring.test_auc) }}
  (95% bootstrap CI {{ "%.2f"|format(m.lead_scoring.test_auc_ci_low) }}–{{ "%.2f"|format(m.lead_scoring.test_auc_ci_high) }}).
  Tier A converts at {{ m.lead_scoring.top_tier_rate|pct }} vs {{ m.lead_scoring.bottom_tier_rate|pct }} for tier D.
  I removed <code>{{ m.lead_scoring.leakage.columns_removed }}</code>: it counts touches <i>after</i> the scoring date and would have inflated AUC by
  +{{ "%.3f"|format(m.lead_scoring.leakage.auc_inflation) }} — a model that looks better in the slide deck and fails in production.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h3>Conversion rate by score tier (test set)</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-lead_tiers"></div></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Points scorecard (logistic regression; +20 points = 2× the odds)</h3>{{ tables.scorecard }}</div>
  </div>
  {% if tables.benchmark %}
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>Robustness: same pipeline on all three published difficulty tiers vs the dataset authors' baselines</h3>{{ tables.benchmark }}
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:12px;margin-top:6px">Published baselines are medians over 5 random seeds; the shipped files are one seed, so ± half the published spread is the honest comparison band.
    On the "advanced" tier (18% missing values, heavy noise) no model is reliable — I report that instead of hiding it.</p></div>
  {% endif %}
  <div class="sowhat"><b>So what:</b> the top 30% of leads (tiers A+B) hold {{ m.lead_scoring.top30_share_of_conversions|pct }} of conversions.
  Route A/B to reps within the hour; send C/D to nurture. The scorecard is readable, so it can live as a HubSpot/Salesforce score property, not a black box.</div>
</section>

<!-- 5 PIPELINE -->
<section id="pipeline">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>5 · Pipeline health</h2><span class="badge synth">SYNTHETIC CRM</span></div>
  <p class="lede">{{ m.pipeline.opportunities|num }} opportunities, {{ m.pipeline.closed_opportunities|num }} closed, win rate {{ m.pipeline.win_rate|pct(1) }}
  (95% CI {{ m.pipeline.win_rate_ci[0]|pct(1) }}–{{ m.pipeline.win_rate_ci[1]|pct(1) }}). {{ m.pipeline.stalled_deals|num }} open deals
  ({{ m.pipeline.stalled_acv|money }}) had no touch for {{ m.pipeline.stalled_after_days }}+ days at their snapshot date.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h3>Funnel</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-funnel"></div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:12px">Win rate by lead source</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-seg_source"></div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:12px">Win rate by industry</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-seg_industry"></div>
      <div class="legend"><span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--series1)"></span>differs from average (CI excludes it)</span><span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--neutral)"></span>within noise</span><span>│ = overall win rate</span></div></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Win rate by rep, with 95% intervals</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-reps"></div>
      <p>Chi-square test across reps: p = {{ "%.2f"|format(m.reps.p_value) }}. The {{ m.reps.min_rate|pct }}–{{ m.reps.max_rate|pct }} spread is
      <b>{% if m.reps.p_value > 0.05 %}consistent with pure chance{% else %}larger than chance{% endif %}</b>.</p>
      <p class="muted">A leaderboard without intervals would have named a "best" and "worst" rep. Coaching decisions need more than noise.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="sowhat"><b>Segments that really differ:</b>
    <ul>{% for s in sig_segments %}<li>{{ s }}</li>{% else %}<li>none at 95% confidence</li>{% endfor %}</ul></div>
</section>

<!-- 6 FORECAST -->
<section id="forecast">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>6 · Forecast: calibrated probabilities vs CRM defaults</h2><span class="badge synth">SYNTHETIC CRM</span></div>
  <p class="lede">Most CRMs ship stage probabilities nobody changes (Negotiation = 90%). On {{ m.forecast.open_deals|num }} open deals ({{ m.forecast.open_pipeline_acv|money }}),
  those defaults forecast <b>{{ m.forecast.forecast_stage_default|money }}</b>. A model trained on {{ m.forecast.closed_deals_for_training|num }} closed deals forecasts
  <b>{{ m.forecast.forecast_model_expected|money }}</b>, with a commit / best-case range of {{ m.forecast.commit_p10|money }} – {{ m.forecast.best_case_p90|money }}
  (20,000 Monte Carlo runs, including uncertainty in the win rate itself).</p>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card"><h3>Distribution of possible bookings</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-forecast"></div></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Calibration (out-of-fold, closed deals)</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-calibration"></div>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:12px">On the diagonal = honest probabilities. Max gap {{ m.forecast.calibration_max_gap|pct }}.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>Forecast by rep (hover for the CRM-default number)</h3><div class="chart" id="fig-forecast_reps"></div></div>
  <div class="sowhat"><b>So what:</b> the stage defaults overstate the number by <b>{{ m.forecast.stage_default_overstatement|pct }}</b>, and
  in {{ m.forecast.prob_reaching_stage_default|pct(1) }} of simulations does the team reach it. Most of the fix is calibration, not AI: deal-level signal here is weak
  (AUC {{ "%.2f"|format(m.forecast.oof_auc) }}), but simply using the real {{ m.forecast.historical_win_rate|pct }} historical win rate instead of 90% removes the miss.
  Close dates are hidden in this dataset, so a back-test by quarter is not possible; the calibration check above is the validation that is possible.</div>
</section>

<!-- 7 QUALITY -->
<section id="quality">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>7 · Data quality report</h2><span class="badge real">REAL firmographics</span><span class="badge synth">SYNTHETIC CRM</span></div>
  <p class="lede">RevOps owns the data. {{ m.quality.checks }} automated checks, each with severity, failed rows, the reason it matters, and who fixes it.
  The state cleaner rescued {{ m.quality.states_fixed|num }} companies with values like "York", "D.C." and "Tx Texas".</p>
  <div class="card">{{ tables.quality }}</div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>State values the cleaner had to handle</h3>{{ tables.state_cleanup }}</div>
</section>

<!-- 8 METHOD -->
<section id="method">
  <div class="sec-head"><h2>8 · Method &amp; sources</h2></div>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h3>Pipeline</h3>
      <p>Hugging Face (pinned commits) → <code>ingest.py</code> (DuckDB streams the 629 MB company file) → SQL models
      <code>staging → intermediate → marts</code> in a DuckDB warehouse → Python analysis (scikit-learn, SciPy) → this static page.
      One command: <code>castiq run-all</code>. CI runs lint, type checks and the test suite on every push.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Datasets</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/bigpictureio/companies-2023-q4-sm">bigpictureio/companies-2023-q4-sm</a> — real firmographics, ODC-BY</li>
        <li><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/markobo/B2B_Sales_data">markobo/B2B_Sales_data</a> — real anonymised B2B deals, CC-BY-4.0</li>
        <li><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/shaypal5/leadforge-lead-scoring-v1">shaypal5/leadforge-lead-scoring-v1</a> — simulated relational CRM, MIT</li>
      </ul></div>
  </div>
</section>

